  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Tricholoma grave is a small to medium-sized mushroom characterized by a grayish-brown cap and white gills, belonging to the Tricholomataceae family. This species is commonly found in New England forests and is considered inedible to poisonous.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap Color and Texture",
      "description": "Gray to brownish-gray cap with a distinctive fibrous or radially streaked appearance, typically 3-7 cm in diameter",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Gill Characteristics",
      "description": "White to pale gray gills that are attached to the stem and relatively crowded, with occasional shorter gills between longer ones",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem Features",
      "description": "Whitish cylindrical stem that may have a slight bulbous base and often shows gray fibrils or staining toward the base",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Tricholoma grave grows in mixed deciduous and coniferous forests throughout New England, typically fruiting in autumn near hardwoods and conifers.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Members of the Tricholoma genus have long been studied for their complex taste receptors and defense chemicals, making them a subject of mycochemical research.",
